  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Participatiegolven shows an early but meaningful scholarly footprint for a 2019 book: it has been cited twice, downloaded 2,061 times, and is openly accessible through repository and publisher routes. The record suggests steady use in architecture, urban planning, and participatory-design research, with additional relevance to policy and sustainability debates.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Participatiegolven is a critical study of participation processes in architecture, urban planning, and spatial planning, and the impact record aligns with that interdisciplinary focus. The strongest signal is usage: the book has been downloaded 2,061 times, indicating substantial reader engagement with the open-access version. That pattern is reinforced by its hybrid open-access status and availability through an institutional repository, which likely supports continued discovery and reuse.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Scholarly uptake is still early, but it is visible. OpenAlex records two citations, both from 2021 articles in planning and design journals. The citing works point to the book\u2019s relevance for participatory planning practice and participatory design, showing that its arguments have already entered adjacent research conversations rather than remaining within a single disciplinary niche.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The surrounding usage context also points to practical relevance. Library holdings and subject classifications place the book within city and town planning, architecture, and city planning, suggesting adoption in collections that serve teaching and research in these fields. The record also indicates alignment with the SDG area of decent work and economic growth, consistent with the book\u2019s policy-facing discussion of participation in the built environment.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the evidence describes a book with clear visibility and a growing scholarly trail, rather than a saturated citation profile. It has attracted attention across planning, design, and humanities-related audiences, and its open availability appears to be supporting that reach. With only two citations so far, the record is best read as an early impact signal that may continue to develop over time.</p>",
